1035 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2624 0 R
/ActualText (Common Use Cases: Image generation, text synthesis, anomaly detection, and tasks requiring an understanding of the full data distribution. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1036 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (How do you handle mode collapse in Generative Adversarial Networks \(GANs\)? )
/E (How do you handle mode collapse in Generative Adversarial Networks \(GANs\)? )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1037 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Mode collapse is a common issue in GANs where the generator repeatedly produces similar outputs, limiting the model's ability to capture the full diversity of the data distribution. Here are several techniques to address mode collapse in GANs: )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1038 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (1. Minibatch Discrimination: )
/E (1. Minibatch Discrimination: )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1039 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2625 0 R
/ActualText (By introducing a measure of similarity between samples within a minibatch, the generator is encouraged to produce varied outputs. The discriminator can access additional features in the minibatch, helping it distinguish similar samples, thus pushing the generator to diversify its outputs. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1040 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (zepanalytics.com)
/E (zepanalytics.com)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1041 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (07)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1042 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (2. Unrolled GANs: )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1043 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2626 0 R
/ActualText (Unrolling allows the generator to anticipate changes in the discriminator by simulating multiple training steps of the discriminator in advance. This discourages the generator from converging to narrow modes, as it learns a broader set of responses from the discriminator. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1044 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (3. Feature Matching: )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1045 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2627 0 R
/ActualText <>
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1046 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (4. Historical Averaging: )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1047 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2628 0 R
/ActualText (This approach penalizes the generator if its parameters deviate significantly from previous values, stabilizing training and encouraging diverse samples by reducing rapid changes that might cause mode collapse.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1048 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ( )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1049 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (zepanalytics.com)
/E (zepanalytics.com)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1050 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (08)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1051 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (5. Ensemble of Discriminators: )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1052 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2629 0 R
/ActualText (Using multiple discriminators forces the generator to produce outputs that satisfy several diverse discriminative models, reducing the likelihood of mode collapse by requiring the generator to fool each discriminator. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1053 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (6. Instance Noise or Label Smoothing: )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1054 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2630 0 R
/ActualText (Adding noise to the inputs or labels can regularize the training, making it harder for the discriminator to overly focus on specific features. This randomness prevents the generator from falling into repetitive outputs. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1055 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (7. Progressive Growing: )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1056 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2631 0 R
/ActualText (This technique gradually increases the resolution of generated images, starting with simpler structures and moving toward more complex patterns. It helps reduce instability and encourages the generator to capture a more complete distribution.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1057 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 766 0 R
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
