/T (1. Understanding Text Representation)
/E (1. Understanding Text Representation)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2654 0 R
/ActualText (Breaking Down Text: Tokenization involves splitting text into smaller units called tokens. These tokens can be words, sub words, characters, or phrases, depending on the tokenization strategy used.  )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2655 0 R
/ActualText (Standardization: It standardizes input data, transforming raw text into a format that can be easily processed by machine learning models.  )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (2. Types of Tokenization )
/E (2. Types of Tokenization )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1193 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2656 0 R
/ActualText (Word Tokenization: In this method, text is divided into words. While straightforward, it can lead to issues with out-of-vocabulary words and morphological variations.  )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (zepanalytics.com)
/E (zepanalytics.com)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1195 0 obj
<<
