/T (How does knowledge distillation benefit LLMs?     )
/E (How does knowledge distillation benefit LLMs?     )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1530 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Knowledge distillation is a process where a smaller model \(the "student"\) learns from a larger, more complex model \(the "teacher"\) to achieve comparable performance with reduced resource requirements. This method is particularly beneficial for large language models \(LLMs\) in several key ways: )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1531 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T ( 1. Model Compression )
/E ( 1. Model Compression )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1532 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2787 0 R
/ActualText (LLMs are often resource-intensive, making them difficult to deploy on standard hardware or in latency-sensitive applications. Knowledge distillation allows for creating a smaller student model that approximates the teacher's behaviour, making it much lighter in terms of memory, computation, and storage. )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1533 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2788 0 R
/ActualText (By transferring knowledge to a compact model, knowledge distillation reduces model size and complexity, making it more practical for real-world deployment. )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1534 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (2. Maintained Performance with Reduced Complexity )
/E (2. Maintained Performance with Reduced Complexity )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1535 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2789 0 R
/ActualText (The student model learns from the teacher's "soft labels" \(i.e., probabilities across all classes\), which carry richer information than hard labels alone. This way, it captures subtle data relationships and nuances learned by the teacher. )
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1536 0 obj
<<
